N,R,S="N","R","S"
SCENES={
"title":[(N,"Operation Gate. The Casino of Chance. Probability and statistics for GATE Data Science and A I, twenty twenty seven."),
(N,"Nine tables, nine twists. Ramu asks why. Somu answers with everyday things, and Python proves it.")],
"cnt":[(N,"Table one. Counting."),
(R,"Why do we count arrangements at all? Probability is about chance."),
(S,"Because when every outcome is equally likely, probability is favourable ways divided by total ways. So you must count both."),
(S,"Order matters, use permutations. Order does not matter, use combinations. Choosing two friends from five is five choose two, which is ten. A handshake among ten people is ten choose two, forty five."),
(R,"What about repeated letters?"),
(S,"Divide by the repeats. The word MISSISSIPPI has eleven letters, four S, four I, two P. Eleven factorial over four factorial, four factorial, two factorial is thirty four thousand six hundred fifty."),
(S,"And stars and bars. Share ten identical sweets among three children. That is twelve choose two, sixty six ways."),
(N,"Twist one. Among only twenty three people, the chance that two share a birthday is already fifty point seven percent. Counting beats intuition.")],
"ax":[(N,"Table two. The axioms of probability."),
(R,"Why do we need axioms? Everyone knows what probability means."),
(S,"Because three simple rules build everything. Probability is never negative. The whole sample space has probability one. And for disjoint events, probabilities add."),
(S,"From these you get the complement rule, and the addition rule. P of A or B equals P of A plus P of B, minus P of A and B. Without the minus, you count the overlap twice."),
(R,"Example?"),
(S,"Roll two dice. The sum seven happens in six of thirty six outcomes, so one sixth. Now the old gambler's puzzle. Roll one die four times. What is the chance of at least one six?"),
(S,"Use the complement. No six in one roll is five sixths. In four rolls, five sixths to the fourth. So the answer is one minus that, which is zero point five one seven seven."),
(N,"Twist two. Disjoint events are never independent, unless one has probability zero. If A happens, B cannot. That is the strongest kind of dependence.")],
"bay":[(N,"Table three. Conditional and Bayesian probability."),
(R,"What does given mean?"),
(S,"It means shrink the world. P of A given B is P of A and B, divided by P of B. You throw away everything outside B and renormalise."),
(S,"Bayes theorem turns it around. P of A given B equals P of B given A, times P of A, over P of B. Use the law of total probability for the denominator."),
(R,"Give me the famous one."),
(S,"A disease affects one percent of people. The test catches ninety nine percent of the sick, and wrongly flags five percent of the healthy. You test positive. What is the chance you are sick?"),
(S,"True positives, zero point zero zero nine nine. False positives, zero point zero four nine five. The ratio is zero point one six six seven. Only one in six."),
(R,"One in six, after a ninety nine percent test?"),
(S,"Yes, because the healthy are so many. Imagine ten thousand people. A hundred sick, ninety nine flagged. Nine thousand nine hundred healthy, four hundred ninety five flagged. Ninety nine out of five hundred ninety four."),
(N,"Twist three. The base rate matters more than the accuracy of the test.")],
"rv":[(N,"Table four. Random variables and common distributions."),
(R,"What is a random variable? It sounds like a variable that is random."),
(S,"It is a rule that turns each outcome into a number. The expected value is the long run average. For a fair die it is three point five. The variance is the average squared distance from that, thirty five over twelve."),
(S,"Binomial counts successes in n trials. Ten coin flips, exactly five heads, is two hundred fifty two over one thousand twenty four, which is zero point two four six."),
(S,"Poisson counts rare events in a window. If three buses arrive per hour on average, the chance of none is e to the minus three, about zero point zero five."),
(R,"And the bell curve?"),
(S,"The normal distribution. About sixty eight percent lies within one standard deviation, ninety five within one point nine six, and ninety nine point seven within three. The exponential describes waiting times and forgets the past."),
(N,"Twist four. The exponential distribution is memoryless. If you have already waited an hour for the bus, the expected remaining wait is the same as when you arrived.")],
"clt":[(N,"Table five. The Central Limit Theorem."),
(R,"Why is everything bell shaped in the end?"),
(S,"Because of the C L T. Add many independent small things, and the total looks normal, whatever the shape you started with."),
(S,"Roll one die. The histogram is flat. Now take the average of thirty dice. The mean stays three point five, but the spread shrinks to sigma over root n, which is zero point three one two."),
(R,"So averages are calmer than single values."),
(S,"Exactly. The standard error falls as one over root n. Four times the data halves the noise."),
(N,"Twist five. The original distribution can be flat, skewed or lumpy. The average of enough of them is still a bell. Even a coin, only zeros and ones, gives a normal after enough flips.")],
"ci":[(N,"Table six. Confidence intervals."),
(R,"What does ninety five percent confidence actually mean?"),
(S,"Not that the truth is in this interval with ninety five percent probability. It means the method, repeated many times, catches the true mean in ninety five percent of the intervals."),
(S,"Take a sample mean of fifty two, sigma ten, n twenty five. The standard error is two. So the interval is fifty two plus or minus one point nine six times two, from forty eight point oh eight to fifty five point nine two."),
(R,"What if sigma is unknown?"),
(S,"Use the sample standard deviation and the t distribution, which has heavier tails. With n sixteen, mean twenty, s four, the t value is two point one three one. The interval is seventeen point eight seven to twenty two point one three."),
(N,"Twist six. A wider interval is more confident, but less useful. To halve the width you need four times the data.")],
"ht":[(N,"Table seven. Hypothesis tests."),
(R,"How do we test a claim?"),
(S,"State a null hypothesis, the boring claim. Compute how surprising the data would be if it were true. That surprise is the p value. If it is below alpha, usually zero point zero five, reject the null."),
(S,"Z test, sigma known. H zero says the mean is one hundred. Sigma fifteen, n thirty six, sample mean one hundred five. Z equals five over two point five, which is two. Two sided p is zero point zero four five five. Reject."),
(S,"T test, sigma unknown. Mean fifty four against fifty, s six, n nine. T equals four over two, which is two, with eight degrees of freedom. P is zero point zero eight. Critical value two point three zero six. Do not reject."),
(R,"Same statistic, different answer?"),
(S,"Yes, because with only nine samples the t curve has heavier tails. Small samples need stronger evidence."),
(S,"Chi squared tests counts. A die rolled sixty times gives eight, nine, twelve, eleven, six, fourteen. Expected is ten each. The statistic is forty two over ten, which is four point two, with five degrees of freedom."),
(S,"The critical value is eleven point zero seven. Four point two is far below it, p about zero point five two. The die is consistent with fair."),
(N,"Twist seven. Not rejecting the null does not prove it true. It only says the data was not surprising enough.")],
"wk":[(N,"Walkthrough. Two algorithms from the lab, line by line."),
(S,"First, Pascal's row. We want four choose two. The list row has three slots, for zero, one, two chosen items. Only the first slot starts at one."),
(S,"For each new item i, update from the back to the front. Every slot adds the slot before it. That is the rule. Ways to choose j equals ways without the new item, plus ways with it."),
(S,"After item one the row is one, one, zero. After item two, one, two, one. After item three, one, three, three. After item four, one, four, six. The last number, six, is four choose two."),
(N,"Main logic. The same list is updated in place, and going backwards avoids using a number we have already changed."),
(S,"Now reservoir sampling. Six items stream past, and we keep three. The first three go straight in."),
(S,"Item three arrives. We draw a random slot number from zero to three. Here the draw is one, which is below three, so forty replaces slot one."),
(S,"Item four draws four, which is not below three, so it is thrown away. Item five draws zero, so sixty replaces slot zero."),
(N,"Main logic. Later items get in with smaller and smaller probability, exactly balancing the head start of the early items. Every item ends with the same chance, k over n.")],
"py":[(N,"Lab one. Python for probability."),
(R,"Can I check the birthday result without trusting the formula?"),
(S,"Simulate it. Make twenty three random birthdays, check for a repeat, and do that twenty thousand times. This is called Monte Carlo."),
(N,"The program prints zero point five one one. The formula said zero point five zero seven. Close, and closer with more trials."),
(R,"And counting, like n choose k?"),
(S,"Build Pascal's triangle in a single row. Each new row adds neighbours. It prints ten, forty five, sixty six, and thirty choose fifteen is one hundred fifty five million, no big factorials needed."),
(N,"Now the C L T. Average thirty dice rolls, five thousand times. The mean is three point four nine seven and the spread zero point three one, matching the theory."),
(N,"Python twist. Always seed the random generator while testing, so your result repeats. Without a seed, every run prints a different number.")],
"dsa":[(N,"Lab two. Randomized algorithms and their complexity."),
(R,"Randomness inside an algorithm? That sounds risky."),
(S,"It is clever. Take reservoir sampling. You must pick k random items from a stream too long to store. Keep the first k, then replace a slot with probability k over i. One pass, order n time, constant memory."),
(N,"Three samples from a million items came out spread across the stream. Every item had the same chance, k over n."),
(R,"Is there another?"),
(S,"Quick select. Pick a random pivot and keep only the side that holds the median. The expected work is n plus n over two plus n over four, and so on, about two n. Not n log n."),
(N,"D S A twist. A random pivot makes the bad case, order n squared, almost impossible. Expectation, the topic of this film, is the tool for analysing algorithms.")],
"end":[(N,"The casino is closed. Counting, axioms, Bayes, distributions, the C L T, intervals, tests, and Python on top."),
(R,"So statistics is honest guessing, with the uncertainty written down."),
(S,"All three films are done. Now go solve the previous year papers. Good luck, Gate topper.")],
}
